SUMMARY:Weedy Wednesdays: Community Stewardship in Toronto's High Park
DESCRIPTION:Edible & Medicinal Plants of Ontario with Stephan Lukacic in Toronto's High Park				\n				\n				\n				\n									Saturday\, July 18\, 2026\, 1-4PM Ages: 18+ years 								\n				\n					\n		\n				\n			\n							\n		\n					\n		\n				\n						\n					\n			\n						\n				\n									NOTE: Pre-registration is required for all our events\, hikes and workshops. We do this to ensure we don’t exceed our capacity for indoor events or the maximum limits for group sizes on High Park’s ecologically sensitive trails. Please register on our website before coming to High Park. We cannot guarantee availability of walk-in spots. If an event is at capacity\, please put yourself on our waitlist. We will notify waitlisted participants if we open up new spots or sessions for our events. Please contact us if you have any questions. 								\n				\n				\n				\n									NOTE: We will not be foraging in High Park. Foraging is not permitted in city\, provincial or national parks. Many nature reserves\, conservation areas and forests also do not allow foraging. Be sure to research foraging and harvesting laws in the different places you visit. When legally engaging in foraging\, we encourage you to not overharvest\, take only what you need and also give back to nature. 								\n				\n				\n				\n									Edible & Medicinal Plants of Ontario – Identification & Use with Stephan Lukacic in Toronto’s High ParkTake an introductory peek into nature’s pantry and pharmacy with popular outdoor educator and avid forager Stephan Lukacic. This fascinating workshop is focused on plant anatomy and identification skills\, and the sustainable harvest of common wild plants for food and medicine. Exploring wild plant ecology\, traditional uses\, and the modern science behind it all\, this comprehensive class will not only teach you how to identify wild plants safely and accurately\, but Steve will share some of his favorite ways of using them\, including delicious recipes and tried-and-true home remedies. The list of plants covered in this workshop includes native\, naturalized\, and invasive species that have both culinary and healing properties\, many of which can be found right in your own backyard. You will receive an 8-page handout covering all the basics of plant identification and ethical foraging\, and read and discuss its contents thoroughly. Then you’ll do a deep dive right onto the trails in High Park — putting the knowledge you’ve acquired directly into practice by observing and identifying wild plants the group encounters in their natural habitat. Come learn everything you ever wanted to know about wild plants and a whole lot more… There’s always time for questions and conversation\, cool people and a bunch of laughs! Note: You will not be foraging for wild plants to take home. This workshop focuses on teaching you how to find and identify wild edible and medicinal plants. Price: $73Ages: 18+ yearsMeeting Location: High Park Forest School\, 375 Colborne Lodge Drive Please bring: A pen or pencilA notebookWeather-appropriate clothingCuriosityReadiness for outdoor learning								\n				\n				\n				\n									\n					\n						\n									Register Now\n					\n					\n				\n								\n				\n					\n		\n					\n		\n				\n						\n					\n			\n						\n				\n					Meet Your Instructor				\n				\n				\n				\n					Stephan LukacicStephan Lukacic is an avid outdoor educator and forager who teaches foraging workshops around Ontario. He also offers guided fishing though his small outfitting business\, and writes for Outdoor Canada Magazine and other publications. He creates hunting\, fishing\, foraging\, and organic gardening content on his Instagram channel @stevie_funfur which is largely focused on sustainability\, self-reliance\, nature\, and living a healthy outdoors lifestyle. He previously worked as a music producer and touring musician who lived and worked in several countries around the world. 				\n				\n				\n				\n									\n					\n						\n									Follow Stephan on Instagram\n					\n					\n				\n								\n				\n				\n						\n					\n			\n							\n		\n				\n			\n						\n				\n					        View this post on Instagram            A post shared by Steve Upin Hali (@stevie_funfur) \n				\n				\n					\n		\n				\n			\n							\n		\n					\n		\n					\n		\n					\n		\n				\n						\n					\n			\n						\n				\n																														\n				\n				\n				\n																														\n				\n				\n						\n					\n			\n							\n		\n				\n			\n						\n				\n							\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Date & Time\n					\n\n					Day: Saturday\, July 18\, 2026Time: 1-4PM\n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Location\n					\n\n					This event will start at the High Park Forest School building located at 375 Colborne Lodge Drive. A number of changes were made to the travel network within High Park as part of the High Park Movement Strategy that might influence your decision to drive to High Park. In case you’re driving we strongly recommend you to park your car at the public parking lot near Grenadier Cafe. We continue to recommend using the TTC or biking as the best way to get to High Park and the Nature Centre. The Nature Centre is situated just a 5-minute walk from the High Park Subway Station. Please note\, High Park is closed to cars on the weekends. If your event is on a weekend\, you will need to park outside the park and walk in\, or take transit. Visit our How To Get Here page to learn more about planning your trips to High Park. \n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Price\n					\n\n					General Admission (Adults): $73\n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Ages\n					\n\n					This event is for adults ages 18 years and above. \n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												What to Bring\n					\n\n					A pen or pencilA notebookWeather-appropriate clothingCuriosityReadiness for outdoor learning\n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Accessibility\n					\n\n					Hike routes through High Park might include some uneven terrain and mild slopes. The High Park Forest School building is not yet an AODA-compliant facility. We try our best to accommodate accessibility needs on a case-by-case basis. Please reach out to us about any accessibility-related questions you might have. \n				\n							\n					\n												\n															\n								\n													\n												Dogs\, Pets and Service Animals\n					\n\n					Dogs and other pets are not permitted in our programs. However\, certified service animals\, such as registered therapy or guide dogs\, are welcome. \n				\n								\n						\n				\n				\n				\n									\n					\n						\n									Register Now\n					\n					\n				\n								\n				\n					\n		\n				\n			\n							\n		\n					\n		\n					\n		\n					\n		\n				\n					\n			\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n		\n				\n			\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n	\n		\n					\n					\n			\n						\n				\n					Registration Policies 				\n				\n				\n				\n									Please ensure you’ve read and understood our registration policies:								\n				\n				\n				\n							\n							\n											\n													\n										General Cancellation Policy\n									\n								\n											\n													\n										Extreme Weather-Related Cancellation Policy\n									\n								\n											\n													\n										Behaviour Policy\n									\n						\n						\n				\n				\n				\n									\n					\n						\n									Read Our Registration Policies
URL:https://highparknaturecentre.com/event/su26-weedy-wednesdays-8/
LOCATION:High Park Forest School\, 375 Colborne Lodge Drive\, Toronto\, ON\, M6P 3K8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Special Events,Stewardship
